{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966807,"nonce":"zMxCLXQ3F4fkJoqdR10Y9PyHy/k=","title":"Wayland \\\"paints once then dead\\\" freeze — idle event loop starves the swapchain acquire;","message":"The months-long Linux/Wayland freeze (\"interaction only happens once, never redrawn\" / \"animates first frames then non-interactive\" / \"responsive for a while, then froze\"). RESOLVED.\n\n**Root cause.** On Wayland (Mesa), once the scene goes static the winit event loop parks in `epoll` and stops presenting. The compositor then stops releasing swapchain buffers, so the next `get_current_texture()` starves for the full ~1s wgpu acquire timeout — the UI thread blocks on the GPU fence (`/proc/\u003ctid\u003e/wchan` = `drm_syncobj_array_wait_timeout`). A single starve is a one-way trap: the UI thread is stuck *inside* the acquire and can't self-drive its way out.\n\n**Fix (two parts, auto-gated to genuine Wayland via `RawDisplayHandle::Wayland` at surface creation → `WindowState.wayland_keep_alive`; X11/macOS/Windows keep the normal dirty-gated path; override `BLINC_KEEP_ALIVE=1/0`):**\n1. **Keep-alive self-drive** — on Wayland, OR `wayland_keep_alive` into the frame loop's `keep_alive` so the dirty-gate never skips, and call `window.request_redraw()` after every `frame.present()`. This is NOT a spin: `pre_present_notify()` (already called before present) arms the compositor frame callback, so winit gates the next `RedrawRequested` on the compositor's `Done` — vsync-paced, steady ~7% CPU, `wchan = ep_poll`. Relies on gotcha wayland pre present notify.\n2. **Reconfigure-on-timeout recovery** — on `SurfaceError::Timeout`, call `surf.configure()` to recreate the swapchain before retrying. This un-sticks the compositor exactly like a manual window resize does (the user's \"resize made it responsive again\" clue). Part 1 alone is fragile; a single slow frame tips it into a starve it can't leave. Part 2 climbs back out.\n\n**Two Heisenbugs that cost months + many false positives — do not repeat:**\n- `WAYLAND_DEBUG=1` **masks** the freeze. Its synchronous per-message stderr logging serializes the present path enough to hide the starve. Every \"responsive\" reading taken with `WAYLAND_DEBUG=1` was a lie. Never measure Wayland responsiveness with it on.\n- The default log level is **INFO** (examples: `EnvFilter::from_default_env().add_directive(Level::INFO)`). INFO-level per-frame latency **tips** the settle-transition into a starve, so the *default* run freezes while `RUST_LOG=warn` runs fine. Always test at the default level, never a hand-picked one.\n\n**Objective diagnostic (cuts through both Heisenbugs):** sample `/proc/\u003cpid\u003e/stat` utime+stime jiffies over 3s with NO `WAYLAND_DEBUG`. ~20 jiffies/3s = healthy vsync 60fps (`wchan ep_poll`); ~300 = busy spin; ~0 = idle-frozen (`ep_poll`) or blocked-frozen (`drm_syncobj_*_wait`). Watch for stray duplicate processes — `pgrep` returning two PIDs breaks the `/proc` read and reads as a false freeze.\n\n**Dead end (removed):** a present-thread (UI renders to an offscreen ring, a dedicated thread owns the Surface and does the blocking acquire+present) only moved the block off the UI thread. It spun on `claim()==None` instead of freezing and still never cycled the display. Same-thread keep-alive + recovery is simpler and actually correct.\n\nDistinct from gotcha wgpu hal 26 0 6 acquire regression (a driver p99 regression fixed by pinning `wgpu-hal = \"=26.0.5\"`; verified the NUC resolved .5, so that was not this bug). Verified on the NUC (Mutter/Wayland, Intel Iris Xe) at default INFO across repeated runs. Optional follow-up: the rebuild-source `tracing::info!` diagnostics in the frame handler are marked \"lower to debug once fixed\" — doing so would cut recovery hiccups further, since INFO latency is the trigger.\n\n**Build gotcha (cost a CI round):** the `wayland-frame-gate` stall-recovery block in windowed.rs is `#[cfg(all(feature = \"wayland-frame-gate\", target_os = \"linux\"))]` and references the acquire's `surf`. It does NOT compile on macOS or under default features — only CI's `--all-features` Linux build exercises it. The keep-alive change moved `surf`'s binding below that block; it compiled everywhere I tested (Mac `windowed`, NUC `windowed`) but failed CI with E0425 (fixed by hoisting `surf` above the block). When editing acquire/present code in windowed.rs, compile-check on Linux with `--features windowed,wayland-frame-gate` before pushing; the Mac cannot exercise that path. (CI's `cargo check --workspace --all-features` also pulls `gio-sys`/GTK via the `rfd`/`dialogs` feature — that needs `libgtk-3-dev` et al., which the NUC lacks but the ubuntu runner installs, so a local NUC `--all-features` check false-fails on gio-sys.)","files":null}]}